                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- COMMON COLUMN -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common Column
        </h2>

        <p>
            In this example, <strong>CourseId</strong> is the common
            column between the Students and Courses tables.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>Students.CourseId = Courses.CourseId</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- BASIC EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Basic INNER JOIN Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            To display student names along with their course names,
            we can use the following query:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT
    Students.StudentName,
    Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
INNER JOIN Courses
    ON Students.CourseId = Courses.CourseId;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- RESULT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Result
        </h2>

        <p>
            INNER JOIN returns only those students whose CourseId
            exists in the Courses table.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>CourseName</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>.NET Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>Java Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>.NET Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>Python Full Stack</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0 mt-4">

            <strong>Notice:</strong>
            Rohan has CourseId 5, but CourseId 5 does not exist in
            the Courses table. Therefore, Rohan is not included in
            the INNER JOIN result.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INNER JOIN WITH WHERE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INNER JOIN with WHERE
        </h2>

        <p>
            INNER JOIN can be combined with a WHERE clause to filter
            the matching records.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT
    Students.StudentName,
    Courses.CourseName
FROM Students
INNER JOIN Courses
    ON Students.CourseId = Courses.CourseId
WHERE Courses.CourseName = '.NET Full Stack';</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns only students enrolled in the .NET Full
            Stack course.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- INNER JOIN WITH ALIAS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INNER JOIN using Table Aliases
        </h2>

        <p>
            Table aliases can make JOIN queries shorter and easier
            to read.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT
    S.StudentName,
    C.CourseName
FROM Students AS S
INNER JOIN Courses AS C
    ON S.CourseId = C.CourseId;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            Here, <strong>S</strong> represents Students and
            <strong>C</strong> represents Courses.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- SELECT SPECIFIC COLUMNS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Selecting Specific Columns
        </h2>

        <p>
            We do not always need to select every column from both
            tables. We can select only the required columns.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT
    S.StudentId,
    S.StudentName,
    C.CourseName
FROM Students AS S
INNER JOIN Courses AS C
    ON S.CourseId = C.CourseId;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- MULTIPLE TABLES -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INNER JOIN with Multiple Tables
        </h2>

        <p>
            INNER JOIN can also be used to combine more than two
            tables.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT
    S.StudentName,
    C.CourseName,
    T.TrainerName
FROM Students AS S
INNER JOIN Courses AS C
    ON S.CourseId = C.CourseId
INNER JOIN Trainers AS T
    ON C.TrainerId = T.TrainerId;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This example demonstrates how multiple related tables
            can be combined using INNER JOIN.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- INNER JOIN VS JOIN -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INNER JOIN vs JOIN
        </h2>

        <p>
            In SQL Server, <strong>JOIN</strong> without a specified
            JOIN type is treated as an <strong>INNER JOIN</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT
    S.StudentName,
    C.CourseName
FROM Students AS S
JOIN Courses AS C
    ON S.CourseId = C.CourseId;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The above query produces the same type of result as an
            INNER JOIN.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    INNER JOIN returns matching records from both
                    tables.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Non-matching records are excluded.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The ON clause defines the relationship between
                    the tables.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    INNER JOIN can be combined with WHERE, ORDER BY
                    and other SQL clauses.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Table aliases make complex JOIN queries easier
                    to read.
                </li>

                <li>
                    JOIN without a specified type means INNER JOIN.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                INNER JOIN combines two or more related tables and
                returns only the rows that have matching values in
                both tables. The ON clause specifies the relationship
                between the tables. INNER JOIN is commonly used to
                retrieve related information from normalized
                database tables.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


